<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Color Fingerprint of Czech Advertising</title>
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com">
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin>
<style>{{ inline_css }}</style>
</head>
<body>
<main>

<header class="masthead">
    <p class="eyebrow">A field study, 2026</p>
    <h1>Color Fingerprint of Czech Advertising</h1>
    <p class="lede">Ten publicly available spots, sampled at one frame per second, clustered in CIE&#8209;LAB color space; the picture that emerges, and the moves it suggests.</p>
    <p class="byline">By {{ author }}. <a href="{{ repo_url }}">Source on GitHub</a>.</p>
</header>

<section class="method">
    <p class="method-label">Method</p>
    <p class="method-body">Frames sampled at one per second from public uploads. Per&#8209;frame palettes via K&#8209;means (k = 5) in canonical CIE&#8209;LAB, where Euclidean distance approximates perceptual color difference. Brand metrics aggregate across all retained frames of each spot.</p>
</section>

<section>
    <div class="section-label"><span class="num">01</span><h2>Per-brand color strips</h2></div>
    <p class="section-intro">Each spot becomes a contact sheet: every frame a vertical column, every column split top to bottom by the K&#8209;means cluster sizes. Dominant colors fill more space; accents stay narrow.</p>
    <div class="strips-list">
{% for row in brand_rows %}
    <div class="brand-row">
        <div class="brand-meta">
            <p class="name"><span class="swatch" style="background: {{ row.anchor_hex }};"></span>{{ row.brand }}</p>
            <p class="sector">{{ row.sector }} · {{ row.n_frames }} frames · arc {{ row.arc_glyph }} {{ row.arc_shape }}</p>
            <div class="brand-spark">{{ row.sparkline_svg | safe }}</div>
        </div>
        {{ row.strip_html | safe }}
    </div>
{% endfor %}
    </div>
</section>

<section>
    <div class="section-label"><span class="num">02</span><h2>Diversity meets saturation tension</h2></div>
    <p class="section-intro">Two independent axes. Quadrants split at the corpus median. Dot color reads each brand's anchor swatch.</p>
    <figure class="scatter-frame">
        {{ scatter_svg | safe }}
        <figcaption class="scatter-cap">Palette diversity (x) vs saturation std across frames (y).</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="sector-ci-block">
        <p class="ci-eyebrow">Sector means with 95% bootstrap CI on saturation std</p>
        {{ sector_ci_svg | safe }}
        <p class="ci-note">Bootstrap resamples brand-level values within each sector. With two to three brands per sector the intervals are wide; the gap between corporate (banking, automotive) and performative sectors is still visible.</p>
    </div>
</section>

<aside class="pull">
    <p class="pull-q">AAA Auto's yellow occupies <span class="num">0.001%</span> of screen-time.</p>
    <p class="pull-cap">Their brand color is a saturated logo yellow; their picture is a dim gray catalog of cars and dealerships. The logo carries the brand. The picture does not.</p>
</aside>

<section>
    <div class="section-label"><span class="num">03</span><h2>Brand color, on-screen color</h2></div>
    <p class="section-intro">Distance, in degrees on the hue wheel, between each brand's anchor swatch and the dominant chromatic color the spot actually puts on screen. Bars fill the swatch; reference at 90&deg; marks the threshold beyond which the two are, perceptually, different colors.</p>
    <figure class="gap-frame">
        {{ gap_svg | safe }}
    </figure>
</section>

<section>
    <div class="section-label"><span class="num">04</span><h2>Where each spot's color mass lives</h2></div>
    <p class="section-intro">A polar histogram per spot. Angle on the wheel is hue (0&deg; red, 120&deg; green, 240&deg; blue); radial length is saturation-weighted density. The tick on the rim marks each brand's anchor hue, in anchor color, so the gap between brand and picture is visible at a glance.</p>
    <div class="radial-grid">
{% for row in brand_rows %}
    <figure class="radial-cell">
        {{ row.radial_svg | safe }}
        <figcaption><span class="rc-name">{{ row.brand }}</span><span class="rc-sector">{{ row.sector }}</span></figcaption>
    </figure>
{% endfor %}
    </div>
</section>

<section>
    <div class="section-label"><span class="num">05</span><h2>Brand similarity, structurally</h2></div>
    <p class="section-intro">Hierarchical clustering on the pairwise Sz&eacute;kely energy distance between every pair of brands' weighted LAB palettes (UPGMA linkage). Spots that join low on the x-axis share a color signature, regardless of sector.</p>
    <figure class="dendrogram-frame">
        {{ dendrogram_svg | safe }}
        <figcaption class="scatter-cap">Energy distance is a kernel-based statistical distance; lower means more similar full-palette distributions.</figcaption>
    </figure>
</section>

<section>
    <div class="section-label"><span class="num">06</span><h2>Patterns</h2></div>
    <div class="patterns">
{% for p in patterns %}
    <article class="pattern">
        <p class="num">{{ "%02d" | format(loop.index) }}</p>
        <div>
            <h3>{{ p.title }}</h3>
            <p class="evidence">{{ p.evidence }}</p>
            <p class="takeaway"><strong>Why it matters</strong>{{ p.takeaway }}</p>
        </div>
    </article>
{% endfor %}
    </div>
</section>

<section>
    <div class="section-label"><span class="num">07</span><h2>Playbook</h2></div>
    <p class="section-intro">Five moves a strategist or creative director can run on next week's pitch.</p>
    <div class="playbook">
{% for move in playbook %}
    <article class="play">
        <p class="play-num">{{ "%02d" | format(loop.index) }}</p>
        <div>
            <h3>{{ move.title }}</h3>
            <p class="play-body">{{ move.body }}</p>
        </div>
    </article>
{% endfor %}
    </div>
</section>

<footer>
    <div>
        <h4>Colophon</h4>
        <p>Set in Bodoni Moda and Manrope.</p>
        <p>Single-file HTML; no JavaScript.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
        <h4>Code</h4>
        <p><a href="{{ repo_url }}">Source on GitHub</a></p>
        <p>MIT for code, CC&#8209;BY&#8209;4.0 for derived data.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
        <h4>Disclaimer</h4>
        <p>Analysis of publicly available video material. Brands and campaigns are referenced for analytical purposes only. No agency attribution is implied.</p>
    </div>
</footer>

</main>
</body>
</html>
